# Thoughts on Ensmallening the BigWeb

This post is full of a number of thoughts I've been mulling, but I was prompted to finally write (some of) it down, in response to this post on the GeminiSpace BBS by CalvusRex:

> Curious, how many of you have actually totally ditched the web, federation, etc for solely smolweb tech? I don't think I can, but really want to.
=>gemini://bbs.geminispace.org/u/CalvusRex/47367

I'm not motivated to ditch the fediverse, I see nothing wrong with it, and continue to derive enormous benefit from it. Everyone who's fedi-fed-up is right to feel that way—I just haven't experienced the same difficulties, myself, and far too many positives. Maybe it helps that the vast majority of feeds I follow, are retro-computer/game related? :D And programmers who aren't big into vibe-coding.

I have no intentions of fully ditching the web, either. It meets many bonafide needs that gemini was deliberately designed not to meet. Nor does Geminispace really appear to be attempting to position itself as a viable replacement for the web (nor for Gopher). It happily lives alongside them, and thus makes a great haven/alternative, but few indeed could remotely consider using it exlusively.

As a retrocomputing/retrogaming fan, many of the more extreme advances in web technlogy that are responsible for empowering corporate platform enslavement and general enshittification, are _also_ responsible for some of my the biggest liberations in how fully and effectively I am able to enjoy, and to share, my biggest passions. I can (and do) integrate editing, debugging, profiling, disassembling, compiling/building, and running of retrocomputing software, all within my browser (and all client-side/open source, so I'm not ensnared), and my productivity while doing so (especially with 8bitworkshop.com) is (at least) an of magnitude better for it. As an occasional hobbyist game developer, and an always-enjoyer of well-written hobbyist games, in-browser games are the most convenient and friendly/least dangerous or cumberson way to share games (and other activities) for others to enjoy. I still believe there is immense value in web "apps", particularly if they are not intrinsically coupled to platforms that have the power to lock you in. (I also think the potential for harm in such tech might be improved if we return to an era of "applet"-style, all-enclosed widgets, rather than blanket permissions for JavaScript/DOM to tread all over an entire webpage, or have naked visibility into every mousemove, keystroke, etc.)

To my mind, the appeal of the Gemini protocol has never actually been the protocol itself. The deliberately scaled-down hypertext format is definitely part of the appeal, but why a new protocol?

Well, the protocol itself does have some important advantages, particularly the greatly-reduced opportunities to spy on the user (via lack of cookies support, lack of user-agent header, etc)— but the _real_ value of having a separate protocol, to me, is not in any particular virtues (or otherwise) of the protocol itself, but merely the requirement to use an entirely different URL scheme, which enables browsers and portals to place brightly-flashing road signs at the departure points, so that you are always made aware when you are leaving the safety guarantees of the smolnet. But this same principle could be adopted, and made both more explicit, and more granular (so that we may all configure/choose our individual balance-points between safety and versatility).

I think (some aspects of) the unhealthy dynamics of the web could be walked back—for instance, by combining "feature profiles" with <a href="..." type="text/html" profile="smol+applet">, to provide guarantees to a browser that (a) the provided link will (say) not, itself, contain any links without explicitly specifying _their_ guarantees, and (b) that a subset of interactivity features (profiles) are used to enable an applet-style thingie (and that the browser (1) may indicate the expanded/potentially undesirable functionality in some manner on the link itself (just as gopher/https/gemini-scheme links are distinguished in a typical smolweb browser), and (b) disable all features not explicitly advertised, upon viewing the content.

Tech features like that, combined with a fiery hatred of all monolithic/centralized platforms, could significantly enhance our freedoms, not just a tiny and heavily-limited corner of the internet, but across the bulk of it.

And don't get me wrong. I love this tiny corner, and believe I will always love it. I don't want it to change, and its rigid and extreme limitations are much of what I will always love it for. But that doesn't mean I want everywhere to be just like it. Just as it doesn't seek to replace Gopher, and Gopher continues to have its place in the smolnet, so too, to my mind, is there room for other perspectives on "smallness" to take shape, without in the least bit damaging or detracting from the value that geminispace brings to the world.

And it is, unfortunately, generally true that the more that we grant flexibility and power to a technology, in order to more perfectly work to our benefit, the more we are granting it the same power to limit, oppress, and enslave us. Even the heavily-limited Gemini protocol provides the technology to authenticate a user to a game server or (say) a BBS, and that same technology (as well as alternatives, like embedded URL tokens) can also be used to place limits on our privacy, or to lock us into a centralized platform. There is no perfect balance to be struck; Gemini (and Gopher) merely strikes it at point where (a) the implementation is straightforward, and (b) there can be quite few complaints about how obtrusive/controlling it is capable of being, over the user.


tags: smolnet
